\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{-2pt}
{\raggedright\footnotesize
trn = trained ($d{=}32$, exposed to $\dt{>}1$ data via subsample ON);
exp$^a$ = trained ($d{=}32$) but never exposed to $\dt{>}1$ data (subsample OFF);
frz$^b$ = frozen random projection ($d{=}32$, \texttt{requires\_grad=False});
--- = removed ($d{=}0$).\\
$^\dagger$ \texttt{reward\_dt\_emb\_detach=True}; $^\ddagger$ all non-KL gradients to \texttt{dt\_emb} detached.\par}
\end{table}

The design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as orthogonal factors, with 008d vs.\ 008f (embedding effect), 008d vs.\ 008a (exposure effect), and 008e\_v3 (imagination-channel control) as key contrasts. Each non-baseline configuration uses 2 seeds; following \citet{agarwal2021precipice}, we report per-seed trajectories to surface stochastic divergence.

\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\paragraph{Evaluation return.}
The primary performance metric: mean return over 10 evaluation episodes, computed every 10K training steps with a deterministic policy.

\paragraph{Value-to-eval ratio.}
We define the value-to-eval ratio $v/e$ as the ratio of the critic's mean imagined value to the evaluation return at each checkpoint. This tracks how well value estimates match realized performance. A ratio near~1 indicates calibrated value predictions; values substantially above~1 indicate systematic overestimation.

\paragraph{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$).}
To control for the baseline's natural $v/e$ dynamics (\eg, the ratio naturally increases during early training as the critic learns), we normalize:
\begin{equation}
\nve_t = \frac{(v/e)_{\text{config},\,t}}{(v/e)_{\text{baseline},\,t}}
\label{eq:nve}
\end{equation}
where $(v/e)_{\text{baseline},\,t}$ is from the single baseline seed (exp\_001, seed~1, 505K steps).  We restrict to a \textbf{35K+ window} ($t \geq 35\text{K}$) because the baseline $v/e$ is near zero in early training, causing spurious $\nve$ inflation.  We use $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ (peak within this window) as a warning threshold; across 12 experimental configurations, this achieves 11/12 accuracy (AUROC${=}0.926$), with one non-CIVO false positive (\S\ref{sec:f6}).  The threshold is sensitive but not perfectly specific to CIVO; mechanistic attribution requires joint analysis with collapse signatures (\S\ref{sec:pathways}).

